      "id": 1,
      "text": "Sam Houston named Prairie Lea after his future wife Margaret Lea, who later became his bride.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prairie_Lea%2C_Texas",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Prairie Lea, Caldwell County's oldest community, was founded on a 1820 land grant from Joe Martin of Gonzales.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prairie_Lea%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Edmund Bellinger, a Battle of San Jacinto veteran, became Prairie Lea's first settler in 1839.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prairie_Lea%2C_Texas",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "By 1853 Prairie Lea boasted a hotel, two stores, and a post office.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prairie_Lea%2C_Texas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "James Hugh Callihan opened Prairie Lea's first store in 1849, sparking local commerce.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prairie_Lea%2C_Texas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "During Reconstruction, Prairie Lea declined, and a fire in the 1870s destroyed part of the town.",
